1
00:00:04,560 --> 00:00:10,530
Time Warner Audio books presents
Rich Dad Poor Dad, written by Robert

2
00:00:10,530 --> 00:00:16,620
Kiyosaki with Sharon l Lecter, CPA,
and read by Steven Hoy, featuring

3
00:00:16,620 --> 00:00:18,630
an introduction by Robert Kiyosaki.

4
00:00:23,670 --> 00:00:26,820
I had two fathers, a
rich one, and a poor one.

5
00:00:27,450 --> 00:00:29,340
One was highly educated and intelligent.

6
00:00:29,715 --> 00:00:34,275
He had a PhD and completed four years of
undergraduate work in less than two years.

7
00:00:34,905 --> 00:00:36,735
The other father never
finished the eighth grade.

8
00:00:37,905 --> 00:00:40,365
Both men were successful in
their careers, working hard.

9
00:00:40,365 --> 00:00:44,955
All their lives, both earned
substantial incomes, yet one

10
00:00:44,955 --> 00:00:46,515
struggled financially all his life.

11
00:00:47,295 --> 00:00:51,255
The other would become one of the
richest men in Hawaii, one guy leaving

12
00:00:51,255 --> 00:00:55,155
tens of millions of dollars to his
family charities and his church.

13
00:00:56,025 --> 00:00:57,465
The other left bills will be paid.

14
00:00:58,335 --> 00:01:01,365
Both men were strong,
charismatic, and influential.

15
00:01:02,055 --> 00:01:06,075
Both men offered me advice, but
they did not advise the same things.

16
00:01:07,035 --> 00:01:10,515
Both men believed strongly in
education, but did not recommend

17
00:01:10,515 --> 00:01:11,715
the same course of study.

18
00:01:12,555 --> 00:01:17,655
If I had had only one dad, I would've
had to accept or reject his advice.

19
00:01:18,735 --> 00:01:23,085
Having two dads advising me offered me
the choice of contrasting points of view,

20
00:01:23,835 --> 00:01:26,295
one of a rich man and one of a poor man.

21
00:01:27,495 --> 00:01:31,785
Instead of simply accepting or
rejecting one or the other, I found

22
00:01:31,785 --> 00:01:35,595
myself thinking more, comparing
and then choosing for myself.

23
00:01:36,525 --> 00:01:42,195
The problem was the rich man was not
rich yet, and the poor man, not yet poor.

24
00:01:43,185 --> 00:01:46,575
Both were just starting out on their
careers and both were struggling

25
00:01:46,575 --> 00:01:49,905
with money and families, but
they had very different points of

26
00:01:49,905 --> 00:01:51,945
view about the subject of money.

27
00:01:52,725 --> 00:01:54,705
For example, one dad would say.

28
00:01:55,215 --> 00:01:57,855
The love of money is the root of all evil.

29
00:01:58,635 --> 00:02:02,175
The other, the lack of money
is the root of all evil.

30
00:02:03,585 --> 00:02:09,044
As a young boy, having two strong fathers
both influencing me was difficult.

31
00:02:10,095 --> 00:02:13,455
I wanted to be a good son and
listen, but the two fathers

32
00:02:13,455 --> 00:02:15,045
did not say the same things.

33
00:02:15,795 --> 00:02:18,855
The contrast in their points of
view, particularly where money

34
00:02:18,855 --> 00:02:22,935
was concerned, was so extreme
that I grew curious and intrigued.

35
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:28,079
I began to start thinking for long periods
of time about what each was saying.

36
00:02:29,100 --> 00:02:32,700
Much of my private time was spent
reflecting, asking myself questions

37
00:02:32,700 --> 00:02:34,739
such as, why does he say that?

38
00:02:35,489 --> 00:02:38,280
And then asking the same question
of the other dad's statement,

39
00:02:39,299 --> 00:02:42,480
it would've been much easier to
simply say, yeah, he's right.

40
00:02:42,480 --> 00:02:43,620
I agree with that.

41
00:02:44,429 --> 00:02:48,630
Or to simply reject the point of
view by saying the old man doesn't

42
00:02:48,630 --> 00:02:50,190
know what he's talking about.

43
00:02:50,924 --> 00:02:54,584
Instead having two dads whom my love
forced me to think and ultimately

44
00:02:54,584 --> 00:02:59,924
choose a way of thinking for myself as
a process, choosing to think for myself

45
00:02:59,984 --> 00:03:04,364
turned out to be much more valuable in
the long run rather than simply accepting

46
00:03:04,364 --> 00:03:06,795
or rejecting a single point of view.

47
00:03:07,935 --> 00:03:12,375
One of the reasons the rich get richer,
the poor get poor, and the middle class

48
00:03:12,375 --> 00:03:17,774
struggles in debt is because a subject of
money is taught at home, not in school.

49
00:03:18,810 --> 00:03:20,940
Most of us learn about
money from our parents.

50
00:03:21,540 --> 00:03:24,209
So what can a poor parent
tell their child about money?

51
00:03:24,510 --> 00:03:27,630
They simply say, stay in
school and study hard.

52
00:03:28,350 --> 00:03:31,950
The child may graduate with excellent
grades, but with a poor person's

53
00:03:31,950 --> 00:03:34,320
financial programming and mindset.

54
00:03:35,370 --> 00:03:37,260
It was learned while the child was young.

55
00:03:38,280 --> 00:03:39,750
Money is not taught in schools.

56
00:03:40,230 --> 00:03:42,870
Schools focus on scholastic
and professional skills,

57
00:03:43,140 --> 00:03:44,490
but not financial skills.

58
00:03:45,330 --> 00:03:49,770
This explains how smart bankers,
doctors, and accountants who are in

59
00:03:49,770 --> 00:03:53,760
excellent grades in school may still
struggle financially all their lives.

60
00:03:54,780 --> 00:03:59,250
Our staggering national debt is due
in large part to highly educated

61
00:03:59,250 --> 00:04:03,690
politicians and government officials
making financial decisions with little

62
00:04:03,690 --> 00:04:05,820
or no training on the subject of money.

63
00:04:06,810 --> 00:04:10,350
I often look ahead to a new millennium
and wonder what will happen when we

64
00:04:10,350 --> 00:04:13,980
have millions of people who will need
financial and medical assistance.

65
00:04:14,625 --> 00:04:18,345
They'll be dependent upon their families
or the government for financial support.

66
00:04:19,035 --> 00:04:22,485
What will happen with Medicare and
social security run out of money?

67
00:04:23,355 --> 00:04:26,925
How will a nation survive if teaching
children about money continues

68
00:04:26,925 --> 00:04:31,755
to be left to parents, most of
whom will be or already are poor?

69
00:04:32,595 --> 00:04:35,985
Because I had two influential
fathers, I learned from both of them.

70
00:04:36,630 --> 00:04:41,219
I had to think about each dad's advice,
and in doing so, I gained valuable

71
00:04:41,219 --> 00:04:45,359
insight into the power and effect
of one's thoughts on one's life.

72
00:04:46,049 --> 00:04:50,309
For example, one dad had a habit
of saying, I can't afford it.

73
00:04:50,940 --> 00:04:53,280
The other dad forbade
those words to be used.

74
00:04:53,760 --> 00:04:54,570
He insisted.

75
00:04:54,570 --> 00:04:56,849
I say, how can I afford it?

76
00:04:57,479 --> 00:04:59,940
One is a statement and
the other is a question.

77
00:05:00,539 --> 00:05:03,359
One lets you off the hook and
the other forces you to think.

78
00:05:04,020 --> 00:05:06,780
My soon to be a rich dad would
explain that by automatically

79
00:05:06,780 --> 00:05:08,940
saying the words, I can't afford it.

80
00:05:09,510 --> 00:05:14,040
Your brain stops working by asking
the question, how can I afford it?

81
00:05:14,520 --> 00:05:15,810
Your brain is put to work.

82
00:05:16,470 --> 00:05:18,660
He did not mean by everything you wanted.

83
00:05:19,020 --> 00:05:21,690
He was fanatical about
exercising your mind.

84
00:05:22,200 --> 00:05:23,970
The most powerful computer in the world.

85
00:05:24,630 --> 00:05:27,720
My brain gets stronger every
day because I exercise it.

86
00:05:28,050 --> 00:05:30,780
The stronger it gets, the
more money I can make.

87
00:05:31,664 --> 00:05:35,445
He believed that automatically
saying, I can't afford it was

88
00:05:35,445 --> 00:05:37,005
a sign of mental laziness.

89
00:05:38,115 --> 00:05:42,284
Although both dads worked hard, I
noticed that one dad had a habit of

90
00:05:42,284 --> 00:05:46,335
putting his brain to sleep when it
came to money matters, and the other

91
00:05:46,335 --> 00:05:48,224
had a habit of exercising his brain.

92
00:05:48,855 --> 00:05:52,275
The long-term result was that
one dad grew stronger financially

93
00:05:52,695 --> 00:05:53,805
and the other grew weaker.

94
00:05:54,435 --> 00:05:57,344
It is not much different from
a person who goes to the gym

95
00:05:57,405 --> 00:05:59,145
to exercise on a regular basis.

96
00:05:59,790 --> 00:06:02,880
Versus someone who sits on
the couch watching television.

97
00:06:03,570 --> 00:06:07,950
Proper physical exercise increases
your chances for health and proper

98
00:06:07,950 --> 00:06:12,600
mental exercise increases your
chances for wealth, laziness

99
00:06:12,719 --> 00:06:15,000
decreases both health and wealth.

100
00:06:16,170 --> 00:06:20,220
My two dads had opposing attitudes
and thought, one dad thought that

101
00:06:20,220 --> 00:06:24,390
the rich should pay more in taxes and
take care of those less fortunate.

102
00:06:24,900 --> 00:06:25,650
The other said.

103
00:06:26,175 --> 00:06:30,495
Taxes, punish those who produce
and reward those who don't produce.

104
00:06:31,155 --> 00:06:31,395
One.

105
00:06:31,395 --> 00:06:35,655
Dad recommended study hard so you
can find a good company to work for.

106
00:06:36,135 --> 00:06:41,745
The other recommended study hard so
he can find a good company to buy one.

107
00:06:41,745 --> 00:06:45,405
Dad said, the reason I'm not
rich is because I have you kids.

108
00:06:46,335 --> 00:06:50,205
The other said, the reason I must
be rich is because I have you kids.

109
00:06:51,284 --> 00:06:55,305
One dad encouraged talking about money
and business at the dinner table.

110
00:06:55,770 --> 00:06:59,790
The other forbade the subject of
money to be discussed over a meal.

111
00:07:00,810 --> 00:07:05,070
One said, when it comes to money,
play it safe and don't take risks.

112
00:07:05,610 --> 00:07:07,800
The other said, learn to manage risk.

113
00:07:08,580 --> 00:07:12,660
One believed our home is our largest
investment and our greatest asset.

114
00:07:13,440 --> 00:07:17,220
The other believed my house is a
liability, and if your house is your

115
00:07:17,220 --> 00:07:19,290
largest investment, you're in trouble.

116
00:07:20,160 --> 00:07:23,715
Both dads paid their bills on
time, yet one paid his bills first.

117
00:07:24,375 --> 00:07:26,055
While the other paid is Bills last.

118
00:07:26,985 --> 00:07:31,515
Being a product of two strong dads allowed
me the luxury of observing the effects

119
00:07:31,935 --> 00:07:34,185
different thoughts have on one's life.

120
00:07:35,235 --> 00:07:38,835
I noticed that people really do shape
their life through their thoughts.

121
00:07:39,735 --> 00:07:44,534
For example, my poor dad always
said, I'll never be rich, and

122
00:07:44,534 --> 00:07:46,245
that prophecy became reality.

123
00:07:47,235 --> 00:07:50,985
My rich dad, on the other hand,
always refer to himself as rich.

124
00:07:51,780 --> 00:07:56,340
He would say things like, I'm a rich
man, and rich people don't do this.

125
00:07:57,090 --> 00:08:01,500
Even when he was flat broke after a
major financial setback, he continued

126
00:08:01,500 --> 00:08:03,150
to refer to himself as a rich man.

127
00:08:04,140 --> 00:08:07,500
He would cover himself by saying,
there's a difference between

128
00:08:07,500 --> 00:08:09,869
being poor and being broke.

129
00:08:10,469 --> 00:08:13,200
Broke is temporary and poor is eternal.

130
00:08:14,190 --> 00:08:17,429
My poor dad would also say,
I'm not interested in money,

131
00:08:17,880 --> 00:08:20,010
or money, doesn't matter.

132
00:08:20,925 --> 00:08:23,235
My rich dad always said, money is power.

133
00:08:24,105 --> 00:08:28,545
The power of our thoughts may never be
measured or appreciated, but it became

134
00:08:28,545 --> 00:08:33,015
obvious to me as a young boy to be aware
of my thoughts and how I expressed myself.

135
00:08:33,435 --> 00:08:37,095
I noticed that my poor dad was poor,
not because of the amount of money he

136
00:08:37,095 --> 00:08:42,345
earned, which was significant, but because
of his thoughts and his actions as a

137
00:08:42,345 --> 00:08:47,115
young boy having two fathers, I became
acutely aware of being careful which

138
00:08:47,115 --> 00:08:49,365
thoughts I chose to adopt as my own.

139
00:08:50,564 --> 00:08:51,525
Whom should I listen to?

140
00:08:52,005 --> 00:08:54,375
My rich dad or my poor dad?

141
00:08:55,125 --> 00:08:58,604
Although both men had tremendous
respect for education and learning,

142
00:08:59,145 --> 00:09:01,545
they disagreed in what they
thought was important to learn.

143
00:09:02,055 --> 00:09:05,234
One wanted me to study hard,
earn a degree, and get a

144
00:09:05,234 --> 00:09:07,094
good job to work for money.

145
00:09:07,515 --> 00:09:10,545
He wanted me to study to become
a professional, an attorney

146
00:09:10,995 --> 00:09:14,265
or an accountant, or to go to
business school for my MBA.

147
00:09:14,895 --> 00:09:18,435
The other encouraged me to study, to
be rich, to understand how money works.

148
00:09:19,200 --> 00:09:20,850
And learn how to have it work for me.

149
00:09:21,720 --> 00:09:22,800
I don't work for money.

150
00:09:23,040 --> 00:09:25,050
Were the words he would
repeat Over and over.

151
00:09:25,470 --> 00:09:26,430
Money works for me.

152
00:09:27,210 --> 00:09:32,220
At the age of nine, I decided to listen
to and learn from my rich dad about money.

153
00:09:32,760 --> 00:09:37,080
In doing so, I chose not to listen
to my poor dad, even though he was

154
00:09:37,080 --> 00:09:39,270
the one with all the college degrees.

155
00:09:39,990 --> 00:09:43,710
Once I made up my mind whom to listen
to, my education about money began,

156
00:09:44,280 --> 00:09:47,725
my rich dad taught me over a period
of 30 years until I was age 39.

157
00:09:48,540 --> 00:09:52,349
He stopped once he realized that I knew
and fully understood what he had been

158
00:09:52,349 --> 00:09:58,410
trying to drum into my often fixed skull
money is one form of power, but what is

159
00:09:58,410 --> 00:10:00,750
more powerful is financial education.

160
00:10:01,349 --> 00:10:05,160
Money comes and goes, but if
you have the education about how

161
00:10:05,160 --> 00:10:09,750
money works, you gain power over
it and can begin building wealth.

162
00:10:10,619 --> 00:10:14,880
The reason positive thinking alone does
not work is because most people went to

163
00:10:14,880 --> 00:10:17,910
school and never learned how money works.

164
00:10:18,210 --> 00:10:21,690
So they spend their lives working
for money because I was only

165
00:10:21,690 --> 00:10:23,100
nine years old when I started.

166
00:10:23,520 --> 00:10:26,700
The lessons my rich dad taught me
were simple, and when it was all

167
00:10:26,700 --> 00:10:31,110
said and done, there are only six
main lessons repeated over 30 years.

168
00:10:31,710 --> 00:10:36,900
This book is about those six lessons
put as simply as possible, as my rich

169
00:10:36,900 --> 00:10:38,760
dad put forth those lessons to me.

170
00:10:39,330 --> 00:10:43,920
The lessons are not meant to be
answers, but guideposts, guideposts

171
00:10:43,920 --> 00:10:46,320
that will assist you and your
children to grow wealthier.

172
00:10:46,650 --> 00:10:50,820
No matter what happens in a world of
increasing change and uncertainty.

